UID:35214-1789745400-1789841700@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:2026 Toronto Colloquium in Medieval Philosophy: A Celebration of Deborah Black
DESCRIPTION:Please join us for the 2026 edition of the University of Toronto Colloquium in Medieval Philosophy. \nProgram\nFRIDAY\, SEPTEMBER 18\nSession I (3:30 – 6:00 pm) \nMartin Pickavé (University of Toronto): Welcome\nStephen Dumont (University of Notre Dame): Deborah as a Scholar\, Friend\, and Colleague\nPeter Adamson (LMU Munich): What’s Truth Got to Do with It? Logic as (Mere) Instrument in Philosophy of the Islamic World \nSATURDAY\, SEPTEMBER 19\nSession II (9:30 am – 12:00 pm) \nGinger Hegedus (Western University): Kalamic Mutations in the Thought of al-Qirqisāni and Ashʿarī\nKara Richardson (Syracuse University): Avicenna on Nature as a Stable Efficient Cause \nSession III (1:45 – 4:15 pm) \nLuis Xavier López-Farjeat (Universidad Panamericana): Ibn Bājja on Imagination and Intellection: An Alternative Interpretation against Averroes’s Reconstruction\nTaneli Kukkonen (NYU Abu Dhabi): Averroes on Past and Future Infinity \nSession IV (4:30 – 6:15 pm) \nStephen Ogden (University of Notre Dame): Avicenna’s Emanationism and Aquinas’s Critique\nDeborah Black: Closing Remarks \nAll sessions are free and open to the public and will be held in Room 100 of the Jackman Humanities Building (170 St. George Street). \nThe colloquium is sponsored by the Department of Philosophy\, the Collaborative Specialization in Ancient and Medieval Philosophy\, the Centre for Medieval Studies\, and St. Michael’s College. \nOrganizers: Reza Hadisi\, Peter King\, Jon McGinnis\, and Martin Pickavé.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/2025-toronto-colloquium-in-medieval-philosophy-a-celebration-of-deborah-black/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building 100
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George,UTM,UTSC
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Medieval-scene-325-x-225.png
END:VEVENT
